export const walmartInterviewAnswers: Record<number, string> = {
  1: 'HashMap stores entries in a power-of-two bucket array. It spreads the key hash, selects a bucket with (capacity - 1) & hash, then compares hash and equals inside that bin; collisions use a list and may treeify, while threshold crossings trigger resize.',
  2: 'hashCode selects the candidate bucket and equals identifies the key within that bucket. Equal objects must have equal hashes; unequal objects may collide. Changing either equality or hash fields while a key is stored makes lookup unreliable.',
  3: 'The keys land in the same bin. HashMap compares their hashes and then equals; unequal keys coexist in a collision chain, which can become a red-black tree when the bin is dense and the table is sufficiently large.',
  4: 'HashMap treats null as a special key with hash zero, so it follows the normal single-key replacement rule and only one null key can exist. Multiple null values are allowed because values do not define map identity.',
  5: 'A collision creates multiple entries in one bin. Modern HashMap starts with linked nodes and may treeify a dense bin; lookup still checks hash and equals, and resizing can redistribute entries but does not eliminate equal hash values.',
  6: 'When size exceeds capacity times load factor, HashMap normally doubles its table and redistributes each bin between its old index and old-index-plus-old-capacity. That O(n) resize makes ordinary insertion amortized O(1), so known bulk loads should be pre-sized.',
  7: 'A 0.75 load factor is an engineering balance: lower values spend more memory to reduce collisions, while higher values save buckets but lengthen bins. It is a default trade-off, not a correctness requirement or a universal optimum.',
  8: 'HashMap is unsynchronized and permits nulls; Hashtable is legacy, synchronizes whole methods, and rejects nulls; ConcurrentHashMap uses CAS and fine-grained bin coordination, rejects nulls, and provides atomic per-key operations without making multi-key workflows transactional.',
  9: 'ArrayList uses a contiguous resizable reference array, giving O(1) indexing and good locality but O(n) middle shifts. LinkedList uses allocated doubly linked nodes, giving O(1) insertion only when the node position is already known, but O(n) lookup and higher memory cost.',
  10: 'HashSet gives average O(1) membership with unspecified order; LinkedHashSet adds insertion-order links; TreeSet uses a red-black tree for sorted navigation in O(log n). Choose by required order and lookup contract, not by habit.',
  11: 'HashSet is backed by a HashMap whose elements are keys mapped to one shared sentinel value. add delegates to put, so uniqueness and correctness depend on stable equals and hashCode; iteration follows the backing map behavior.',
  12: 'A safe HashMap key has immutable fields participating in equals and hashCode, a correct equality contract, and a well-distributed hash. Records and immutable value objects are good candidates; database entities with generated mutable identity require special care.',
  13: 'Fail-fast iterators detect some structural interference and may throw ConcurrentModificationException; this is best-effort bug detection, not synchronization. Snapshot or weakly consistent concurrent iterators tolerate updates with explicitly different visibility semantics, so “fail-safe” is an informal label.',
  14: 'Comparable defines the type’s single natural order through compareTo; Comparator is an external, composable ordering strategy. Both must be antisymmetric and transitive, and sorted maps and sets treat compare(a,b) == 0 as key equality.',
  15: 'Natural ordering usually rejects null. A comparator can state the policy explicitly with nullsFirst or nullsLast around a non-null comparator; the same policy must be consistent across sorting, persistence, and API output.',
  16: 'ArrayList index access and hash lookup are average O(1); linked or array searches are O(n); balanced-tree lookup and updates are O(log n); heap peek is O(1) and offer/poll O(log n). State worst cases, resize costs, and memory locality as well as Big-O.',
  17: 'PriorityQueue is an array-backed binary min-heap. offer appends and sifts up, poll replaces the root and sifts down, and peek is O(1); only the head is guaranteed minimal, so iteration is not sorted and mutable priority fields are unsafe.',
  18: 'Use EnumMap for keys from one enum type: ordinal-indexed storage is compact and declaration ordered. Use IdentityHashMap only when reference identity, not equals, is the deliberate contract, such as object-graph traversal; it is wrong for ordinary domain keys.',
  19: 'Collection is the root interface for groups of elements, Collections is a utility class for algorithms and wrappers, and Arrays is a utility class for array operations. Map belongs to the collections framework but is not a Collection subtype.',
  20: 'An immutable collection cannot be structurally changed, but its elements may still be mutable. A defensive copy breaks container aliasing at a boundary; use copyOf plus immutable element types when callers must not mutate published state.',
  21: 'WeakHashMap holds keys through weak references, so an entry may disappear after its key has no other strong reference and GC runs. It is suitable for auxiliary metadata tied to object lifetime, not deterministic caches or authoritative business state.',
  22: 'A shallow copy creates a new outer object but shares referenced nested objects; a deep copy recursively duplicates the mutable object graph. Deep copying needs an explicit ownership boundary because cycles, identity, cost, and external resources make generic copying unsafe.',
  23: 'ArrayDeque is a resizable circular array supporting efficient operations at both ends and should normally replace legacy Stack. Stack extends Vector, exposes an obsolete inheritance model, and synchronizes individual methods without making compound workflows safe.',
  24: 'A view such as subList is a live window backed by the original collection, not an independent copy. Changes can flow both ways, range constraints still apply, and structural changes through another path can invalidate the view or its iterator.',
  25: 'HashMap records a key under the bucket chosen from its original hash. If equality or hash fields change, lookup searches a different bucket or fails comparisons, leaving an entry visible during iteration but logically unreachable; remove before mutation or use immutable keys.',
  26: 'Java can run work through Thread, Runnable, Callable, ExecutorService, CompletableFuture, or virtual-thread executors. In production prefer task submission to managed executors so concurrency, queuing, cancellation, shutdown, and observability are explicit.',
  27: 'Runnable returns no result and cannot declare checked exceptions; Callable returns a value and may throw checked exceptions. ExecutorService can execute either, while submit returns a Future that carries completion, result, cancellation, or failure.',
  28: 'Thread safety means concurrent calls preserve the component’s invariants and visibility guarantees for every allowed interleaving. It comes from immutability, confinement, synchronization, atomic operations, or concurrent structures and must include compound actions, not only individual methods.',
  29: 'Every Java object can act as a monitor. synchronized acquires that monitor before entering, releases it on exit even after exceptions, provides mutual exclusion, and establishes happens-before from unlock to a later lock on the same monitor.',
  30: 'Deadlock occurs when threads form a cycle of waiting for resources, commonly through inconsistent lock order. Prevent it with global ordering, reduced lock scope, timed acquisition, or single ownership; diagnose it with thread dumps and monitor information.',
  31: 'In livelock, threads keep reacting and make no useful progress; in starvation, one thread rarely obtains CPU or a contested resource. Backoff, fair coordination where justified, bounded retries, and contention metrics address them differently.',
  32: 'A reentrant lock lets its owning thread acquire the same lock again while maintaining a hold count. This allows synchronized methods to call other synchronized methods on the same object without self-deadlocking.',
  33: 'Both provide mutual exclusion and visibility. synchronized is simpler and automatically released; ReentrantLock adds timed or interruptible acquisition, conditions, and optional fairness but requires disciplined try/finally unlock and is not inherently faster.',
  34: 'ReadWriteLock permits multiple readers or one writer when reads dominate and are sufficiently long. StampedLock adds optimistic reads and stamps but is non-reentrant and easier to misuse; validate the stamp and benchmark real contention before choosing either.',
  35: 'ConcurrentHashMap reads generally avoid locking, empty-bin insertion uses CAS, and contended structural updates coordinate per bin; resize is cooperative. It provides per-key atomic methods and weakly consistent iteration, not nulls, global snapshots, or multi-key transactions.',
  36: 'volatile gives visibility and ordering for reads and writes of that variable, but does not make compound increments atomic. Happens-before is the JMM relation guaranteeing that prior actions become visible, established by locks, volatile access, thread start/join, and safe publication rules.',
  37: 'The Java Memory Model defines which writes a thread is allowed to observe and how operations may be reordered. Safe publication creates a happens-before edge using final-field construction, volatile, locking, static initialization, or a concurrent container so readers see fully initialized state.',
  38: 'ExecutorService separates task submission from thread management. A pool takes tasks from a queue, assigns workers, exposes Future results, and requires explicit overload, rejection, cancellation, exception handling, and graceful shutdown policies.',
  39: 'Pool sizing follows workload and downstream capacity: CPU-bound work starts near available cores, while blocking work may need more threads based on wait/compute ratio. Validate with throughput, queue time, utilization, tail latency, and resource limits rather than a fixed formula alone.',
  40: 'CompletableFuture models a completion graph whose stages run inline or asynchronously. Async methods without an executor normally use the common ForkJoinPool; production code should pass a workload-specific executor for blocking work and handle failure, timeout, cancellation, and context propagation.',
  41: 'ThreadLocal associates a value with the current thread. Pool threads outlive requests, so failing to remove a value retains objects and can leak one request’s context into another; use try/finally remove or scoped context propagation.',
  42: 'StackOverflowError occurs when a thread exhausts its stack, usually through unbounded recursion, cyclic calls, or very deep frames. Capture the repeating stack trace, fix the termination/invariant, or replace recursion with iteration; increasing stack size only postpones a logic defect.',
  43: 'A Java memory leak is unwanted reachability: GC cannot reclaim objects still reachable from roots such as static maps, threads, listeners, class loaders, or ThreadLocals. Confirm growth with GC evidence and heap dumps, then trace retaining paths rather than blaming GC.',
  44: 'The heap stores objects and is GC-managed; each thread stack stores frames and local execution state; metaspace stores class metadata in native memory; direct buffers allocate off-heap native memory. Diagnose them with different metrics because heap settings do not cap every process-memory area.',
  45: 'A tracing collector finds live objects from GC roots, reclaims unreachable memory, and may move survivors while coordinating through barriers and safepoints. Collector choice trades throughput, latency, footprint, and CPU; use allocation, pause, occupancy, and promotion evidence.',
  46: 'String is immutable so its value and hash can be safely shared, pooled, cached, used as a map key, and passed through security-sensitive APIs. Operations create new strings; the referenced object never changes.',
  47: 'Make the class final or prevent unsafe subclassing, set private final fields in a validating constructor, expose no mutators, defensively copy mutable inputs and outputs, and ensure contained objects are immutable. Safe construction must not leak this.',
  48: 'String is immutable; StringBuilder is mutable and fastest for thread-confined concatenation; StringBuffer synchronizes methods for legacy shared use. Prefer StringBuilder locally and explicit coordination if a whole multi-step shared operation must be atomic.',
  49: 'A functional interface has one abstract method, allowing a lambda or method reference as its implementation; default and Object methods do not count. @FunctionalInterface documents and compiler-checks the intent, while generic specializations such as IntFunction avoid boxing.',
  50: 'A lambda supplies behavior for a functional-interface target and may capture effectively final variables; a method reference is shorthand when an existing method matches the target signature. Both compile to invokedynamic-based call sites rather than requiring an anonymous source class.',
  51: 'Intermediate stream operations such as map and filter are lazy and build a pipeline; a terminal operation such as collect or reduce triggers traversal and consumes the stream. Stateful operations, side effects, encounter order, and parallel execution affect cost and correctness.',
  52: 'Optional explicitly models a possibly absent return value and supports composition; it is not a universal replacement for null fields, parameters, entities, or serialization. Use it mainly at return boundaries and avoid get without an absence strategy.',
  53: 'Default methods add inheritable interface behavior and support compatible evolution; implementing classes may override them and conflict rules resolve multiple defaults. Static interface methods are namespaced utilities and are not inherited as instance methods.',
  54: 'Generics provide compile-time type safety through parameterized types and bounds. Java erases most type arguments at runtime, inserting casts and bridge methods, so you cannot instantiate T, overload only by generic arguments, or reliably test List<String> with instanceof.',
  55: 'Checked exceptions must be declared or handled and suit recoverable conditions callers can reasonably address; unchecked exceptions usually represent programming, validation, or invariant failures. Choose by recovery contract, not by wrapping every checked exception mechanically.',
  56: 'try-with-resources closes AutoCloseable resources in reverse declaration order. If the body and close both fail, the body exception remains primary and close failures are attached as suppressed exceptions, preserving the full diagnostic chain.',
  57: 'A singleton guarantees one instance per class loader, not globally across processes. Enum singletons handle serialization safely; initialization-on-demand holder is lazy; dependency-injection singleton scope improves testability. The instance must still be thread-safe.',
  58: 'Double-checked locking avoids locking after initialization, but the instance field must be volatile. Volatile prevents publication of a reference before constructor writes are visible and makes the second null check observe completed initialization.',
  59: 'Use Builder when construction has many optional parameters, validation, readable named steps, or immutable output. Avoid it for simple values, and ensure build performs cross-field validation and defensive copying instead of permitting partially valid objects.',
  60: 'Strategy encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind one contract and selects one through composition, configuration, or runtime context. Use it when behavior varies independently; avoid a strategy hierarchy when a simple function or small conditional is clearer.',
  61: 'Spring Framework provides the container, DI, AOP, MVC, data, and transaction foundations. Spring Boot builds on it with opinionated auto-configuration, starters, executable packaging, external configuration, and production tooling; Boot reduces setup but does not replace Spring concepts.',
  62: 'SpringApplication prepares the environment, determines application type, creates and refreshes the ApplicationContext, loads bean definitions, runs post-processors, instantiates non-lazy singletons, starts the embedded server, publishes events, and invokes runners. Failure during refresh closes the context.',
  63: 'The container instantiates a bean, injects dependencies, runs aware callbacks and BeanPostProcessors, invokes initialization callbacks, exposes the proxied result, and later runs destruction callbacks for managed scopes. Creation and exposed proxy identity may differ.',
  64: 'Dependency injection gives an object its collaborators instead of letting it construct or locate them. This separates wiring from behavior, exposes required dependencies, supports substitution in tests, and lets the container manage lifecycle and cross-cutting proxies.',
  65: 'Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit, permits final fields, enables plain unit construction, and prevents partially initialized instances. Setter injection is suitable for truly optional reconfiguration; field injection hides dependencies and complicates tests.',
  66: 'Spring resolves by required type, then considers qualifiers, @Primary, bean name, priority/order, and generic metadata. If ambiguity remains it throws NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException; collection injection intentionally supplies all matching beans.',
  67: '@Primary supplies the default candidate when several beans match a type; @Qualifier requests a specific semantic candidate at the injection point. Qualifier is more explicit and overrides the need to rely on a global default.',
  68: 'All three are component stereotypes and register detected beans. @Service communicates application/domain service intent; @Repository marks persistence adapters and enables exception translation; @Component is the general stereotype. They do not create separate runtime scopes.',
  69: 'Circular dependencies mean A needs B while B needs A, often signaling mixed responsibilities. Remove the cycle by extracting a third collaborator, changing ownership, publishing an event, or redesigning boundaries; @Lazy or setter injection only masks it.',
  70: 'Singleton is one bean per application context; prototype creates on each lookup; request, session, and application scopes follow web lifecycles. Scope affects state sharing, proxying, destruction, and thread safety, so singleton beans should normally be stateless.',
  71: 'Boot discovers auto-configuration imports, evaluates conditional annotations against classpath, properties, beans, and application type, then registers matching definitions. User beans usually back off auto-configuration, and the condition report explains why a configuration matched.',
  72: 'A starter is a curated dependency set that activates a capability consistently; it contains little or no runtime logic itself. Conditional annotations make auto-configuration apply only when classes, properties, resources, or missing beans satisfy stated conditions.',
  73: 'Spring builds an ordered Environment from configuration data, environment variables, system properties, command-line arguments, and profile-specific sources. Profiles select bean/config variants; typed @ConfigurationProperties with validation is safer than scattered string lookups.',
  74: 'Spring AOP uses proxies to apply cross-cutting advice at bean method boundaries, commonly for transactions, security, metrics, and caching. Proxy limits matter: self-invocation, private methods, final methods/classes, ordering, and exception behavior can bypass assumptions.',
  75: 'DispatcherServlet receives a request, finds a HandlerMapping, applies interceptors, invokes through a HandlerAdapter, resolves return values or a view, and delegates exceptions to resolvers. Filters and Spring Security run around this servlet boundary.',
  76: '@Controller is an MVC stereotype whose methods may return view names; @RestController combines @Controller and @ResponseBody so return values are serialized through message converters by default. Both use the same request-mapping infrastructure.',
  77: 'Filters wrap the servlet request before Spring MVC and suit security, correlation, and low-level HTTP concerns; interceptors surround mapped controllers; AOP surrounds Spring bean method execution. Choose the boundary that actually owns the concern and understand async dispatch.',
  78: 'Actuator exposes health contributors and Kubernetes-oriented groups. Liveness should report whether the process must restart; readiness whether it can accept traffic, including critical dependencies; startup probes protect slow initialization. Do not make liveness depend on every remote service.',
  79: 'Spring’s usual transaction management is proxy-based, and private methods cannot be invoked through an overriding proxy boundary. Put the transactional operation on an externally invoked public service method, or use TransactionTemplate/AspectJ when that model is unsuitable.',
  80: 'A call from one method to another on this uses the target directly rather than re-entering the proxy, so transactional advice is skipped. Move the operation to another bean, invoke through an intentional proxy boundary, or use programmatic transactions.',
  81: 'Prefer extracting the transactional unit into a separate service and call it through its injected interface. TransactionTemplate is appropriate for local programmatic boundaries; self-proxy injection is possible but couples business code to proxy mechanics and is rarely the cleanest design.',
  82: 'A static method is not an instance invocation on a Spring proxy, so proxy-based @Transactional advice does not apply. Keep transaction orchestration on managed instance methods or wrap static utility work inside a transactional service boundary.',
  83: 'Use TransactionTemplate when the boundary must be programmatic, conditional, repeated in chunks, or explicit around a callback, especially when proxy interception cannot apply. Keep the callback small and avoid mixing remote calls into a long database transaction.',
  84: 'Propagation defines how a method joins or creates transactions: REQUIRED joins or creates, REQUIRES_NEW suspends and creates, NESTED uses a savepoint where supported, SUPPORTS/MANDATORY/NOT_SUPPORTED/NEVER express other participation rules. Each choice affects connection demand and rollback behavior.',
  85: 'Read uncommitted permits dirty reads; read committed prevents them; repeatable read also stabilizes rereads; serializable provides the strongest isolation through ordering/conflicts. Actual MVCC and locking behavior is database-specific, so select from anomalies the business invariant cannot tolerate.',
  86: 'A read-only transaction communicates intent, establishes one persistence context/connection boundary, and may enable flush or database optimizations. It is valuable for consistent lazy loading and routing but should be measured rather than assumed faster.',
  87: 'No. In Spring and many databases readOnly is primarily a hint; Hibernate may alter flush behavior, but writes can still occur or fail only at the database depending on configuration. Enforce no-write architecture separately and test the actual database behavior.',
  88: 'Place a transaction around one cohesive local business use case and the state that must commit atomically. Keep it at the service boundary, short, and free of slow remote calls; distributed consistency uses outbox, saga, or another protocol rather than one database transaction.',
  89: 'By default Spring rolls back on RuntimeException and Error, not checked exceptions. Configure rollbackFor/noRollbackFor only when the business contract requires it, and remember that catching an exception inside the boundary can prevent the proxy from seeing it.',
  90: 'Use SELECT FOR UPDATE or pessimistic locking when a transaction must serialize access to existing rows and conflict probability is high. Keep lock order and duration controlled, ensure supporting indexes, and handle timeouts/deadlocks; absent rows require a different uniqueness strategy.',
  91: 'Optimistic locking allows concurrent work and detects conflicts at update time through a version, fitting low-contention workloads; pessimistic locking blocks competitors earlier, fitting short high-conflict transitions. Choose using conflict rate, retry cost, latency, and deadlock risk.',
  92: '@Version adds the version value to update/delete predicates. If another transaction changed it, zero rows are updated and Hibernate throws an optimistic-lock exception; the application must retry from fresh state or return a conflict, never blindly overwrite.',
  93: 'N+1 occurs when one query loads parent rows and later lazy access issues one query per parent for related data. Detect it with SQL/query counts and fix the use-case query, because changing every association to eager merely moves or multiplies the cost.',
  94: 'A fetch join retrieves the needed association in one JPQL query; EntityGraph selects fetch paths declaratively; batch fetching groups lazy loads into IN queries. Choose per use case and beware collection pagination, cartesian multiplication, duplicate parents, and memory growth.',
  95: 'An entity is transient before persistence, managed while attached to a persistence context, detached after that context ends, and removed after delete scheduling. Dirty checking applies to managed entities; merge copies detached state into a managed instance and returns that instance.',
  96: 'The first-level cache is the mandatory persistence-context identity map: one managed instance per entity identity within a session. It avoids repeated loads and enables dirty checking, but can consume memory in large batches, requiring flush and clear.',
  97: 'Second-level cache shares entity/collection data across sessions for a factory. It can reduce reads but introduces invalidation, staleness, cluster-coherence, and memory concerns; cache only stable, read-heavy data with a strategy matching update consistency.',
  98: 'Yes, depending on cache strategy, invalidation timing, external database writes, and transaction isolation. The first-level cache can also return an already managed older instance; freshness-critical reads may need refresh, cache bypass, or a different consistency design.',
  99: 'Second-level cache stores entity and collection state by identity. Query cache stores query result identifiers/metadata and normally relies on second-level cache for the entities; it is useful only for stable repeated query shapes and has broader invalidation cost.',
  100: 'Lazy loading defers association data until access. Access outside an open persistence context can throw LazyInitializationException; fix it by fetching exactly what the use case needs inside the transaction or mapping to a DTO, not by keeping sessions open accidentally.',
  101: 'GET retrieves and is safe/idempotent; POST creates or triggers a command and is not inherently idempotent; PUT replaces a resource idempotently; PATCH partially changes it with defined patch semantics; DELETE removes it and should be idempotent at the contract level.',
  102: 'PathVariable identifies a resource within the route, such as /orders/{id}; RequestParam modifies filtering, sorting, pagination, or an optional operation. Put stable resource identity in the path and avoid action-style URLs when HTTP semantics already express the operation.',
  103: 'Require an idempotency key scoped to caller and operation, atomically reserve it with a request fingerprint, execute the business transition once, and persist the terminal response for replay. Concurrent duplicates must wait for or observe the same result, not run again.',
  104: 'Store a unique tuple such as tenant, operation, and key with request hash, state, response/status, resource ID, and expiry. Insert atomically before side effects; reject key reuse with a different payload and replay the original response for completed duplicates.',
  105: 'Prefer keyset pagination using a stable unique order such as createdAt plus id, encode the last key in an opaque cursor, and query with a matching composite index. Offset pagination becomes slower and unstable under concurrent inserts at large offsets.',
  106: 'Favor additive compatible changes, tolerant readers, explicit deprecation, and contract tests. Use path/header/media-type versions only for genuinely incompatible behavior, run versions in parallel through a measured migration window, and never silently reinterpret an existing field.',
  107: 'REST is an architectural style using HTTP resources, verbs, caching, and representations; SOAP is a protocol with XML envelopes, WSDL contracts, and mature WS-* standards. REST is often simpler for web APIs, while SOAP may fit strict enterprise contracts and message-level standards.',
  108: 'A RESTful API exposes resource-oriented URIs, uses HTTP methods and status codes correctly, keeps requests stateless, provides cache semantics where valid, and exchanges representations. Hypermedia is part of the formal style but many practical HTTP APIs implement only a subset.',
  109: 'Use one machine-readable error envelope with stable code, safe message, field violations, correlation ID, and optional retry metadata. Map domain failures consistently to HTTP status, avoid leaking stack traces or sensitive data, and document errors in the API contract.',
  110: 'OAuth2 defines delegated authorization flows and token acquisition; JWT is one possible self-contained access-token format. Validate issuer, audience, signature, expiry, and scopes, rotate keys, keep tokens short-lived, and enforce resource ownership after authentication.',
  111: 'The gateway authenticates and applies coarse edge policy, while every service validates trusted identity context and enforces operation- and object-level authorization for the data it owns. Defense in depth is necessary because internal paths, events, and configuration can bypass UI or gateway assumptions.',
  112: 'The owning service alone has the authoritative resource and business context needed for object-level authorization. Trusting only the gateway enables confused-deputy and direct/internal-call vulnerabilities; propagate identity safely but re-check permission at the trust boundary.',
  113: 'The gateway owns routing, TLS termination, authentication, coarse scopes, quotas, and edge observability. A service owns business authorization, tenant/resource ownership, field rules, and audit decisions; the gateway cannot infer authorization from URL visibility alone.',
  114: 'Rate limiting controls request volume; CORS tells browsers which origins may read cross-origin responses; CSRF prevents a browser from sending unwanted authenticated state-changing requests. They address different threats and none replaces authentication or authorization.',
  115: 'Identify resources such as books, inventory, carts, orders, and payments; define lifecycle transitions, ownership, validation, idempotency, pagination, and errors. Separate catalog reads from transactional checkout, use durable consistency patterns, and secure every customer/admin boundary.',
  116: 'Relational databases provide schemas, joins, constraints, and strong transactions; NoSQL families optimize particular access, scale, or data-shape needs with different consistency/query trade-offs. Choose from invariants and access patterns, not a generic scale claim, and accept operational complexity explicitly.',
  117: 'Atomicity commits all or none, consistency preserves declared invariants, isolation controls concurrent anomalies, and durability preserves committed results after failure. ACID describes a transaction guarantee; application correctness still requires correct boundaries, constraints, and retry handling.',
  118: 'An index is an auxiliary ordered/search structure that accelerates matching, ordering, and joins. It costs storage, cache space, and write maintenance; low-selectivity, redundant, or badly ordered indexes can be ignored or slow writes, so verify with query plans and workload metrics.',
  119: 'A partition key determines physical placement and distribution, shaping scalability and which queries are local. A secondary index creates an alternate lookup structure; it does not necessarily change placement and can amplify storage/write cost or perform poorly across partitions.',
  120: 'Normalization reduces duplication and update anomalies through related tables; denormalization duplicates derived data to optimize known reads. Start normalized for transactional truth, denormalize deliberately for measured access patterns, and define ownership, update propagation, and repair.',
  121: 'Both are mature relational databases. PostgreSQL emphasizes standards, advanced SQL, extensibility, rich types, and sophisticated query features; MySQL has a broad ecosystem and common operational familiarity. Select using required semantics, extensions, replication, tooling, and team evidence.',
  122: 'MVCC lets readers use versioned snapshots while writers create new row versions, reducing read/write blocking. Isolation determines snapshot lifetime and anomaly protection; long transactions retain old versions and increase cleanup/storage pressure, while write conflicts still require handling.',
  123: 'Start with the exact slow query, parameters, frequency, and latency distribution; inspect EXPLAIN ANALYZE, rows estimated versus actual, scans, joins, sorts, locks, and I/O. Then fix query shape, indexes, statistics, or data model and verify end-to-end rather than forcing an index blindly.',
  124: 'Prefer a relational database when multiple related writes must preserve constraints atomically, ad-hoc joins/reporting matter, or consistency failures have high business cost. Strong ACID does not prevent scaling, but schema and transaction design must match contention and availability goals.',
  125: 'Cassandra distributes writes by partition key, appends to a commit log and memtable, later flushes immutable SSTables, and avoids random in-place updates. Replication and sequential I/O provide throughput, while compaction, partition design, and consistency settings determine the real cost.',
  126: 'Cassandra trades flexible joins and arbitrary queries for query-first denormalized tables and distributed availability. Tunable consistency balances read/write acknowledgments; eventual repair, tombstones, compaction, hot partitions, and limited transactions must fit the domain.',
  127: 'Cache-aside loads on miss and updates the database plus invalidates the cache, which is simple but permits stale windows. Write-through updates cache and backing store through one path, improving read freshness but adding write latency and still requiring failure/recovery semantics.',
  128: 'Stampede is many callers rebuilding one expired hot key; penetration is repeated lookup of absent keys; avalanche is many keys or a cache tier failing together. Use request coalescing, negative caching, jittered TTLs, capacity isolation, and database protection with bounded fallbacks.',
  129: 'Define the database as authority, update it transactionally, then invalidate or version cache entries through durable change propagation. Use TTL as a safety net, tolerate duplicate/out-of-order events, and monitor stale reads and invalidation lag; deletion and concurrent fills need fencing/version checks.',
  130: 'MongoDB eventual consistency is acceptable when stale reads are bounded and do not violate money, inventory, authorization, or workflow invariants, such as feeds or analytics. Choose read/write concerns per operation and provide monotonic or session guarantees where user experience requires them.',
  131: 'Brokers store partition replicas for topics; producers append records, usually with a key; consumers pull ordered partition logs and groups divide partitions among members. Offsets track group progress, while retention decouples storage from consumption and enables replay.',
  132: 'Partitions are Kafka’s unit of parallelism, ordering, replication, and storage distribution. More partitions increase potential throughput and consumers, but add metadata, open files, rebalance work, and make future key/order changes operationally significant.',
  133: 'Kafka orders records only within one partition by append offset. Preserve business order by sending related records with a stable key to the same partition and by controlling retries/concurrency; there is no global topic order across partitions.',
  134: 'The producer hashes a non-null key to choose a partition, so a stable aggregate ID keeps that aggregate’s events together. Key skew can create hot partitions, and changing partition count can remap keys, so capacity and ordering migration need planning.',
  135: 'A consumer group is one logical subscription whose members share topic partitions. Kafka assigns each partition to at most one active consumer in that group, enabling parallel processing; a different group receives its own independent copy and offsets.',
  136: 'Not concurrently during a stable assignment: one partition is owned by one consumer within a group. During rebalances ownership transfers, so incorrect offset/side-effect handling can still cause duplicate processing across the transition.',
  137: 'Extra consumers remain idle because a partition cannot be split among two active consumers in the same group. Increasing consumers beyond partition count adds failover capacity but not steady-state parallelism.',
  138: 'Consumers receive multiple partitions, so one worker processes more streams and may build lag. Add consumers up to partition count or improve per-record work, while checking key distribution because equal partition counts do not guarantee balanced load.',
  139: 'Lag is the difference between the log end and the group’s committed/processed offset. It grows when input exceeds processing, records block, downstreams slow, rebalances repeat, GC pauses, or a hot partition dominates; diagnose by partition and processing stage.',
  140: 'First remove bottlenecks and make processing bounded; then scale consumers up to partitions, batch safe operations, tune fetch/poll, or add partitions with ordering review. Do not merely increase concurrency if the database or remote dependency is already saturated.',
  141: 'commitSync waits and retries according to client behavior, giving a clear result but adding latency. commitAsync avoids blocking but callbacks can complete out of order; track the highest safe processed offset and use synchronous commit during revoke/shutdown where appropriate.',
  142: 'Processing may have succeeded while progress was not recorded, so records can be redelivered after restart or rebalance. Treat commit failure as an at-least-once condition: make effects idempotent, observe callbacks, and avoid advancing beyond incomplete work.',
  143: 'Classify transient versus permanent failure, apply bounded retry with backoff without blocking the consumer indefinitely, then route terminal records to a DLT with payload reference, error, attempt, and correlation metadata. Operators need inspect, repair, replay, and audit procedures.',
  144: 'A dead-letter topic isolates records that exhausted policy so healthy traffic continues. Replay only after fixing the cause and confirming schema, ordering, idempotency, authorization, and destination capacity; preserve the original key and audit every replay.',
  145: 'Assume at-least-once delivery. Atomically claim an event ID or business transition with the domain write, make state transitions conditional, and treat repeated success as success; in-memory sets are insufficient across restarts and replicas.',
  146: 'Use a stable event/message ID and persist a unique inbox record in the same local transaction as the business update. If the ID already exists, acknowledge without repeating effects; downstream calls need their own idempotency keys and state machines.',
  147: 'The broker assigns an idempotent producer a PID and tracks monotonically increasing sequence numbers per partition. Retries with the same PID/sequence are deduplicated within the producer session, preventing retry duplicates in Kafka but not duplicate business requests or external effects.',
  148: 'Kafka transactions can atomically publish records and consumed offsets across Kafka partitions for read-process-write flows when configured correctly. They do not make database, HTTP, payment, or arbitrary external side effects atomic, and consumers must use read_committed.',
  149: 'Retries can let later records overtake an earlier failed record when multiple requests are in flight or when retry topics are used. Preserve order with idempotence and compatible in-flight settings, partition-pausing retry, or an explicit per-key sequencing/state design.',
  150: 'Membership, subscription, or timeout changes revoke and reassign partitions. Stop new work, finish or cancel in-flight processing, commit only safe offsets, and initialize the new assignment; cooperative rebalancing reduces disruption but does not remove duplicate risk.',
  151: 'Monitor broker availability, under-replicated/offline partitions, ISR changes, controller health, disk/network, request latency, throughput, errors, consumer lag by partition, rebalance frequency, processing latency, and DLT/retry growth. Alerts should connect to recovery actions and SLOs.',
  152: 'Kafka is a retained partitioned log optimized for replay, streaming, and high throughput; RabbitMQ is a message broker optimized for routing, queues, acknowledgments, and per-message delivery patterns. Choose from retention/replay, ordering, routing, latency, and operational needs.',
  153: 'Use a synchronous API when the caller needs an immediate result, validation, or read and both sides can share the latency/availability budget. Use Kafka for durable decoupled state propagation and asynchronous workflows; many systems use HTTP for commands/queries and events for outcomes.',
  154: 'Kafka provides retained replayable logs; ActiveMQ provides traditional queues/topics and rich enterprise messaging semantics; MQTT is a lightweight pub/sub protocol for constrained or intermittently connected devices. Compare delivery model, routing, ordering, retention, client environment, and operations.',
  155: 'Replication and acknowledgments protect partition data; partitions distribute throughput; consumer groups parallelize processing; idempotent producers and durable offsets support recovery. Reliability still depends on keying, min ISR, unclean election policy, idempotent consumers, and tested replay.',
  156: 'A monolith deploys one application boundary and simplifies transactions, debugging, and operations; microservices split ownership and deployment but add network, consistency, observability, and platform cost. Choose services around proven independent change/scale boundaries, not team fashion.',
  157: 'Retain a monolith when the domain or team is small, boundaries are still evolving, independent scaling/deployment offers little value, and operational maturity is limited. A modular monolith can enforce boundaries now and preserve a lower-risk extraction path later.',
  158: 'Database ownership prevents hidden coupling, cross-service schema dependencies, and shared transaction assumptions. Other services consume APIs or events; duplicated read models are acceptable when ownership, freshness, compatibility, and repair are explicit.',
  159: 'A service consumes events relevant to its owned state or decisions, based on a documented contract and business capability. It should not subscribe merely to query another service’s database indirectly; review schema compatibility, key/order, idempotency, replay, and deletion/privacy obligations.',
  160: 'A command asks a specific owner to attempt an action and may be rejected; an event states an immutable fact that already happened and can have many subscribers. Naming an instruction as an event hides coupling and ambiguous failure ownership.',
  161: 'APIs handle immediate commands, validation, and queries where a caller needs a response; events propagate committed facts and decouple downstream reactions. A common flow accepts a command synchronously, commits local state plus outbox, then publishes an outcome event.',
  162: 'Model the saga as durable idempotent state transitions. Retry transient failures, time out missing outcomes, compensate already completed business steps where valid, route poison events, and expose correlation plus operator-driven recovery rather than leaving an implicit half-complete workflow.',
  163: 'The order must not wait forever. Persist a deadline, retry or query through an approved recovery path, and on expiry transition idempotently to a failed/cancelled state and compensate prior steps; late replies must be recognized and handled by state/version rules.',
  164: 'Timeouts bound uncertainty, retries handle transient loss, and idempotency makes retries safe under at-least-once delivery. Together they still require durable state, backoff, attempt limits, correlation, and terminal recovery so retry storms do not become the failure.',
  165: 'Database rollback erases uncommitted local changes within one transaction. Compensation is a new auditable business action that semantically reverses a previously committed step, may fail or be partial, and must itself be idempotent and observable.',
  166: 'Transactional outbox writes domain state and an event-intent row atomically in one database transaction. A relay later publishes and marks it, so crashes cannot lose intent; duplicates remain possible, requiring idempotent consumers, lag monitoring, cleanup, and recovery.',
  167: 'An inbox persists a consumed message ID, often in the same transaction as the local state change. A unique constraint makes duplicate delivery a no-op; define retention and replay windows and do not confuse transport ID with the business transition key.',
  168: '2PC coordinates prepare and commit across participating transactional resources for atomicity but adds blocking, coordinator, latency, and availability costs. Saga uses committed local transactions plus messages and compensation, improving autonomy while exposing intermediate states and recovery complexity.',
  169: 'In phase one the coordinator asks participants to prepare and durably promise they can commit; in phase two it records and sends commit or rollback. Failures between phases can leave locks/resources waiting for coordinator recovery, which is the major availability cost.',
  170: 'Patterns include transactional outbox/inbox, reservation with expiry, TCC, escrow, workflow orchestration, event sourcing, and reconciliation. They solve different consistency problems; choose from invariant, ownership, reversibility, latency, and failure-recovery requirements rather than naming a pattern alone.',
  171: 'CQRS separates write commands/models from read models when their scaling or shapes differ. Event sourcing stores state as an append-only event history and rebuilds projections; they are independent choices and add versioning, replay, projection lag, and operational complexity.',
  172: 'Propagate one trace context plus stable business IDs through HTTP, events, logs, metrics, and state records. Persist workflow status and deadlines, make transitions idempotent, provide searchable timelines and replay/repair tools, and avoid high-cardinality or sensitive telemetry.',
  173: 'Docker packages an application and dependencies into an immutable image and runs isolated processes with namespaces and resource controls, reducing environment drift. It does not provide orchestration, durability, or VM-level isolation, so image security and runtime limits remain necessary.',
  174: 'Docker builds and runs containers; Kubernetes schedules and reconciles containerized workloads across nodes, providing desired state, networking, rollout, configuration, and recovery. Kubernetes commonly uses OCI images built by Docker-compatible tooling but no longer depends on Docker Engine.',
  175: 'Kubelet runs configured probes and reports container/pod status; controllers compare actual with desired state and replace failed pods. Readiness removes endpoints without restart, while liveness can restart a stuck container; node and workload failures also drive eviction/reconciliation.',
  176: 'Startup protects slow initialization before other probes apply; readiness controls whether traffic is sent; liveness decides whether to restart the container. Use cheap local checks, conservative thresholds, and never make liveness fail solely because a downstream dependency is unavailable.',
  177: 'A ReplicaSet maintains the requested count of pods matching a selector, creating or deleting them as state changes. Deployments normally own ReplicaSets to provide declarative rollout history; users rarely manage ReplicaSets directly.',
  178: 'A Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually scales it up while scaling the old one down according to maxSurge and maxUnavailable. Readiness gates traffic, progress deadlines expose stalls, and rollback selects a previous template revision.',
  179: 'Requests drive scheduling and reserve capacity; limits cap usage. CPU over limit is throttled, memory over limit can cause OOM kill, and poor requests cause packing or starvation problems. Size from observed working set, throttling, latency, and node headroom.',
  180: 'HPA periodically compares observed metrics with targets and adjusts desired replicas within bounds, most commonly using CPU utilization relative to requests. Metric delay, stabilization, startup behavior, pod readiness, and downstream capacity must be tuned to avoid oscillation.',
  181: 'Services provide stable virtual IP/DNS over changing pod endpoints selected by labels. EndpointSlices track ready backends and kube-proxy or an eBPF dataplane distributes traffic; this is connection/request distribution, not application-aware global load balancing by default.',
  182: 'Kubernetes marks the pod terminating, removes it from service endpoints, runs preStop, sends SIGTERM, waits the grace period, then sends SIGKILL. Applications must stop admission, drain in-flight work, close consumers, and finish within the configured deadline.',
  183: 'TKGI is VMware Tanzu Kubernetes Grid Integrated, a platform that provisions and manages Kubernetes clusters using BOSH with integrated networking, load balancing, upgrades, and lifecycle operations. Explain the exact deployed version because product naming and components evolve.',
  184: 'OpenShift is a Kubernetes distribution with an integrated platform opinion: stricter security defaults, Operators, routes, registry/build and developer workflows, and enterprise lifecycle tooling. Kubernetes APIs remain foundational, while OpenShift adds policy and platform services.',
  185: 'Clarify seat-hold duration, traffic peaks, and payment semantics; partition inventory by show, use authoritative seat state with atomic conditional holds and expiry, then confirm through an idempotent saga. Prevent double booking with constraints/state versions and design queueing, recovery, and hot-show scaling.',
  186: 'Separate media upload/storage/CDN from feed metadata. Model follow relationships, fan out on write for ordinary users and on read for celebrities, rank and paginate with stable cursors, and handle privacy, deletion, cache invalidation, backfill, and eventual consistency.',
  187: 'Store tweets durably, build home timelines with a hybrid fan-out strategy, use a social graph and ranking service, and paginate by stable cursor. Address celebrity fan-out, edits/deletes, cache hierarchy, abuse, regional availability, and asynchronous timeline repair.',
  188: 'An API gateway is the external entry point for routing, TLS, authentication, coarse authorization, quotas, transformations, and observability. Keep business logic and object ownership in services, avoid a single unbounded bottleneck, and design timeout and failure behavior.',
  189: 'A load balancer selects healthy backends using an algorithm such as round-robin, least connections, or hashing, often terminating transport and performing health checks. Discuss L4 versus L7, connection draining, retries, locality, stickiness, and avoiding retry amplification.',
  190: 'Vertical scaling gives one instance more resources and is simple but bounded and failure-concentrated. Horizontal scaling adds instances for capacity and resilience but requires statelessness or partitioned state, load balancing, coordination, and data scaling.',
  191: 'Define the identity and limit dimension, choose token bucket for controlled bursts or sliding-window accuracy, and enforce atomically in a shared or partitioned store. Specify distributed consistency, clock behavior, headers, fail-open/closed policy, hot keys, and observability.',
  192: 'CAP says that during a network partition a distributed system cannot guarantee both linearizable consistency and every request’s availability. The real choice is operation-specific: reject/queue a checkout transition to protect inventory, while serving a stale catalog read may be acceptable.',
  193: 'Start with user-facing SLOs, instrument latency distributions, throughput, errors, saturation, and critical dependency spans, then load test realistic traffic and profile bottlenecks. Compare against a baseline, change one variable, and verify correctness as well as speed.',
  194: 'Use latency, traffic, errors, and saturation as golden signals, then define SLIs at the customer boundary and SLOs with an error budget. Add domain signals such as checkout completion and event age; alerts should be actionable, symptom-based, and tied to runbooks.',
  195: 'Estimate peak requests per second, per-request CPU/memory/I/O, concurrency from latency, storage growth, and redundancy headroom. Divide required throughput by measured safe instance capacity, then constrain by downstream bottlenecks and validate with load tests plus failure scenarios.',
  196: 'Begin with requirements and quality attributes, draw service/data ownership, then narrate one critical request and one failure path end to end. Explain security, consistency, deployment, observability, capacity, trade-offs, and evidence from your contribution; avoid listing technologies without decisions.',
};
